# A .pubignore takes precedence over the .gitignore *in the same directory*,
# so this file must repeat everything package-root .gitignore excludes —
# otherwise .dart_tool/, build/ and friends would end up in the published
# archive. Nested .gitignore files (example/.gitignore) still apply on their
# own, so they are not repeated here.

# --- mirrored from .gitignore ---
*.class
*.log
*.pyc
*.swp
.DS_Store
.atom/
.buildlog/
.history
.svn/
migrate_working_dir/

*.iml
*.ipr
*.iws
.idea/

/pubspec.lock
**/doc/api/
.dart_tool/
.flutter-plugins
.flutter-plugins-dependencies
build/

# --- publish-only exclusions ---
# Every runtime dependency is Android/iOS-only, so the example's desktop and
# web runners are dead weight in the archive. They stay in git (the example is
# still a normal `flutter create` project locally) — this only trims what
# ships to pub.dev.
example/web/
example/linux/
example/macos/
example/windows/

# Local coverage output — regenerated by `flutter test --coverage`.
coverage/

# Internal working docs. These shipped to pub.dev up to and including 2.3.0 —
# doc/ is larger than lib/, and it carried doc/audit/ (security audits naming
# the VIP-bypass weaknesses that are still open, with file:line), doc/task/
# backlogs, and design notes for an unrelated app. Consumer-facing setup lives
# in README.md; the rest belongs in git, not in the published archive.
# doc/screenshots/ must stay — `screenshots:` in pubspec.yaml points at it.
doc/*
!doc/screenshots/

# The test suite serves this repo's CI, not consumers, and pana does not score
# it either way.
test/
